I agree, we can come back to the naming situation later, as long as the concept is locked-in. On the threading situation: Okay so basically what I am hearing is create new threads on demand, aside from the baseline we setup by default on initialization? Then we also have logic that can confirm the number of physical cores the CPU has available, and set the process-wide budget here. How does that sound? Maybe we offer the ability to stretch that to the number of logical cores if we need more too? And where did you land on that loop stacking idea? Basically you could issue multiple loop-frame methods to one of the loop threads and it executes more than one loop? Considering that its possible the process-wide budget is only 4. The signature for these loops (and the workers) is always the same as shown in my notes:

WorkerThread accepts -> void doSomething();
LoopThread accepts -> void doSomething(ThreadLoopContext*);

And to clear up the confusion around involuntary thread execution termination, when user-code issues a loop thread, their loop method is supposed to be written in such a way that it is a single pass of the loop. They do not write a loop themselves, because then yes, I could not stop their execution. Instead the users loop is wrapped by top-level logic (similar to the try-catch immediately before beginning to wait for user worker functions) that does the actual looping and it can check the '`ThreadLoopContext`' just like user-code can within their loop. If a thread was asked to stop looping and user-code disregards, I can still use that top-side logic as a means to end the thread, because ultimately it is Chevron logic doing the looping holding the thread open. User-code simply provides the "frame" of the loop, which can be stateful, that is user-code's business. Although of course, if a user writes an infinite loop within the loop there is nothing I can do about that, but that shouldn't be the case ever, that's not how this is meant to be used. I also agree on user-code having access to the memory scope, but maybe something might wedge between the two because the API for these resource constructs might be too revealing for my taste with user-facing code. I'd like to hear your response to these.

